 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 6,
   "text": "THE RUDSTON MONOLITH. At about 7.6 metres, the tallest standing stone in Britain, and probably as much again below ground. It was raised around 4,000 years ago from gritstone dragged here from the coast near Scarborough, some ten miles off. The Normans built All Saints right beside it, which tells you who was here first.",
   "plaque": "wolds_rudston_monolith",
   "title": "The Rudston Monolith"
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 15,
   "text": "THE GYPSEY RACE. A chalk stream that flows past Rudston to Bridlington, except when it doesn't. For years at a time its bed lies dry, then the water rises out of the chalk. Wolds folk called it a woe water: when it ran, they said, bad times were coming, from failed harvests to war.",
   "plaque": "wolds_gypsey_race",
   "title": "The Gypsey Race"
  },
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "RED KITES. Look up: a forked tail and a mewing whistle. Red kites were wiped out in England by the 1870s, poisoned and shot as vermin. Young birds from the Chilterns, where kites of Spanish stock had been set free in 1990, were released at Harewood near Leeds from 1999, and their young have spread east over the Wolds. Kilham folk now count them from the farm gate.",
   "plaque": "wolds_red_kites",
   "title": "Red Kites over Kilham"
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "WOLDGATE. David Hockney, born in Bradford, came back to paint the Wolds from 2004, often outdoors and in all weathers. 'Bigger Trees Near Warter' (2007) was painted on fifty canvases at once, and 'The Arrival of Spring in Woldgate' (2011) partly on an iPad. Woldgate is an old Roman road from York to Bridlington.",
   "plaque": "wolds_hockney",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Hockney's Wolds"
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "WHARRAM PERCY. Up on the western Wolds lie the humps and bumps of a deserted medieval village, its roofless church still standing. Its people were slowly turned out around 1500 as the land went over to sheep, which paid better. Archaeologists dug there every summer for forty years.",
   "plaque": "wolds_wharram_percy",
   "title": "Wharram Percy"
  },
  {
   "x": 54,
   "y": 25,
   "text": "DEW POND. Chalk drinks the rain straight down, so the Wolds have few streams. Farmers dug shallow ponds and lined them with puddled clay to catch the rain for their sheep. Despite the name, almost none of the water is dew.",
   "plaque": "wolds_dew_pond",
   "title": "Dew Pond"
  },
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "THE YORKSHIRE WOLDS WAY. A National Trail of 79 miles, from Hessle under the Humber Bridge to Filey Brigg on the coast, all chalk dales and big skies. Follow the acorn waymarks, and the sheep, who know a short cut.",
   "plaque": "wolds_wolds_way",
   "title": "The Yorkshire Wolds Way"
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 25,
   "text": "WOLDGATE. South: Hull, Hessle Road and the Humber. North-east: Rudston. West: York."
  }
 ],
